<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>

However, the above assertion seems to incorrectly capture the intended specification. The correct interpretation should be that for every valid index from the specified index to the old size of the list, the element at the current position should equal the old element at the next position. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>

But since the element at the new index should be the newly inserted element and the rest should shift, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert \forall int i; index<i && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This captures the fact that all elements from the index+1 to the old size are shifted right by one position. The element at the specified index is the newly inserted element, so we start checking from index+1.

Therefore, the final correct assert statement is:

<code>assert \forall int i; index<i && i<\old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>
